## 1. Wireless and Cellular Data Networks

### Wireless Data Networks

Mobile devices commonly connect to networks using wireless technologies instead of physical Ethernet cables.

The two major types of wireless connectivity covered in this module are:

- Wi-Fi
- Cellular networks

### Wi-Fi

Wi-Fi allows mobile devices to connect to a local wireless network and access resources such as the internet.

A Wi-Fi connection typically uses a wireless access point or wireless router.

#### Wi-Fi Configuration

When manually configuring a wireless connection, the device may require:

- Network name (SSID)
- Security type
- Password or encryption key
- IP configuration, if manually configured

Most home and public Wi-Fi networks automatically assign IP configuration using DHCP.

### Cellular Networks

Cellular networks allow mobile devices to communicate over a mobile carrier's network.

Unlike Wi-Fi, cellular connectivity does not require the user to be connected to a local wireless router.

Cellular connectivity can provide:

- Internet access
- Voice communication
- SMS messaging
- Mobile data services

### Cellular Communication Standards

Cellular technology has evolved through multiple generations.

| Generation | General Characteristics |
|---|---|
| 2G | Digital cellular communication and SMS |
| 3G | Mobile internet and improved data communication |
| 4G LTE | Faster mobile data and improved internet connectivity |
| 5G | Higher speeds, lower latency, and increased network capacity |

The exact performance of a cellular connection depends on factors such as signal strength, network congestion, coverage, and the device.

